generated_solution:
First, let's find angle $BAC$. Since the sum of the angles in a triangle is 180 degrees, we can find angle $BAC$ as:
\[ \text{angle } BAC = 180 - 70 - 50 = 110 - 50 = 60 \]

The line $AD$ is perpendicular to $BC$, and we know the following angles:
   \begin{align*}
   \text{angle } BAD &= 70 \\
   \text{angle } DAC &= 50 \\
   \text{angle } CAE &= 30
   \end{align*}

Let's look at the relationship between $A$ and $E$, the two ends of the diameter of the circle. We can see that triangle $ADE$ is a right triangle.

We have:
\[ \text{angle } ADE = 90 \]

Also, since the line $DE$ is a diameter of the circle, the angle subtended by the diameter at the circumference is 90 degrees:
\[ \text{angle } DEC = 90 \]

Now consider the quadrilateral $DECO$. Since the angles at $D$ and $C$ are both 90 degrees, and $O$ is the center of the circle, we can find the angle $DEO$:
   \begin{align*}
   \text{angle } DEO &= 360 - 90 - 90 - 50 \\
   &= 130
   \end{align*}

Since triangle $ADE$ is a right triangle, we can find angle $DAE$:
   \begin{align*}
   \text{angle } DAE &= 180 - 130 \\
   &= \boxed{20}
   \end{align*}

