Find the smallest positive value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 0$.

generated_solution:
We can expand the determinant along the first row:
\begin{align*}
   \begin{vmatrix} p & q & r \\ q & r & p \\ r & p & q \end{vmatrix} &= p\begin{vmatrix} r & p \\ p & q \end{vmatrix} - q\begin{vmatrix} q & p \\ r & q \end{vmatrix} + r\begin{vmatrix} q & r \\ r & p \end{vmatrix} \\
   &= p(rq - p^2) - q(q^2 - pr) + r(pq - r^2) \\
   &= prq - p^3 - q^3 + pqr + pqr - r^3 \\
   &= 3pqr - p^3 - q^3 - r^3
\end{align*}

Now, note that $p,$ $q,$ $r$ are the roots of the cubic polynomial $x^3 + mx + n = 0,$ so we have
\begin{align*}
   p + q + r &= 0, \\
   pq + qr + pr &= m, \\
   pqr &= -n.
\end{align*}

Also, recall that $a^3 + b^3 + c^3 - 3abc = (a + b + c)(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 - ab - ac - bc)$.

We can substitute $a = p,$ $b = q,$ $c = r$ to get $p^3 + q^3 + r^3 - 3pqr = (p + q + r)(p^2 + q^2 + r^2 - pq - pr - qr)$.

But $p + q + r = 0,$ so $p^3 + q^3 + r^3 - 3pqr = 0,$ which means $p^3 + q^3 + r^3 = 3pqr$.

Therefore, our original determinant is equal to
\begin{align*}
   3pqr - p^3 - q^3 - r^3 &= 3pqr - (p^3 + q^3 + r^3) \\
   &= 3pqr - 3pqr \\
   &= \boxed{0}
\end{align*}

We can write out the expression $a*b+b*c+c*a$ and then simplify it.
\begin{align*}
a*b+b*c+c*a &= a^2+b^2+b^2+c^2+c^2+a^2\\
&= 2a^2+2b^2+2c^2\\
&= 2(a^2+b^2+c^2)
\end{align*}
Now, we can use the fact that $a+b+c=0$ to simplify this expression further. Squaring both sides of $a+b+c=0$ gives us
\begin{align*}
(a+b+c)^2 &= 0\\
a^2+b^2+c^2+2ab+2bc+2ca &= 0\\
a^2+b^2+c^2+2(ab+bc+ca) &= 0
\end{align*}
Substituting $ab+bc+ca=-12$ into this equation, we get
\begin{align*}
a^2+b^2+c^2+2(-12) &= 0\\
a^2+b^2+c^2-24 &= 0\\
a^2+b^2+c^2 &= 24
\end{align*}
Therefore, we have
\begin{align*}
a*b+b*c+c*a &= 2(a^2+b^2+c^2)\\
&= 2(24)\\
&= \boxed{48}
\end{align*}

